Title: update-publist resolves HAL credentials from the keystore
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T13:46Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`skills/update-publist` reads `HAL_ID` and `HAL_PASSWORD` from the ambient
environment; its SKILL.md even documents a presence probe
(`bash -c ': "${HAL_ID:?not selected from this cwd}"'`) because the credential
vanishes depending on the cwd's `KEYS=` line. That probe is a workaround for
0360, and point-of-use resolution removes the need for it.

Child of 0942. Same pattern as 0393.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Both HAL variables resolve from `~/.config/keys/hal.env` at point of use
- [ ] The cwd-dependent presence probe is removed from SKILL.md, with the
      resolution documented in its place
- [ ] Fails loud and named on an unresolvable credential
- [ ] Fake-sentinel test, never a real credential
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine pass 2026-09-16 — the skill has no code to put a resolver in

`skills/update-publist/` contains exactly one file, `SKILL.md`. No script, no
test. The ticket's own exit criteria ("fails loud and named", "fake-sentinel
test") cannot be asserted against Markdown instructions, so this ticket must
create the file it needs.

**Shape:** a small dedicated script in the skill directory, on the model of
`skills/external-peer-review/peer_review.py`, which resolves the same tension by
bundling real code a test can invoke. SKILL.md then gains a fixed point-of-use
step instead of restating shell isolation mechanics as prose the agent must
reproduce correctly from memory on each run.

**Two shapes rejected, with reasons:**
- Sourcing `skills/reviewers/reviewers.sh` to reuse `_keystore_file_for` would
  drag its whole CLI surface (list/request/harvest, panel parsing, seat
  orchestration) into a HAL deposit. Wrong coupling.
- Sourcing `~/.config/keys/hal.env` wholesale because "it only holds two
  variables anyway" executes it as shell code in the current, non-isolated
  shell, putting both values into that shell's environment and every child it
  spawns afterwards — reinstating exactly the residency 0942 exists to remove.
  The file's current size does not move the trust boundary. Extract two named
  variables in an `env -i` subshell, as the reference implementation does.

**Two variables, one provider** changes the call count, not the mechanism:
`_keystore_value(file, name)` is already parameterized on the name. This skill
runs once per interactive deposit, so two subshells cost nothing worth
optimizing into a multi-variable API.

**The cwd probe** (`bash -c ': "${HAL_ID:?not selected from this cwd}"'` in
SKILL.md) is load-bearing nowhere else — a repo-wide search finds it only in
that file. It is advisory documentation that fires only if the agent remembers
to run it. Point-of-use resolution bypasses the `KEYS=` selection layer
entirely, so the 0360 failure it guarded stops being reachable, and the
mandatory "fails loud" check replaces it while also catching cases the probe
never covered (missing or malformed `hal.env`, not just a cwd mismatch).

**Antipattern scan:** one pressure named and dismissed. Two instances of a
~15-line idiom (`reviewers.sh` and this new script) will tempt extraction of a
shared bash library. Premature: the rule of three is unmet, 0943 is Python and
could not consume it anyway, and it would push this ticket into `reviewers.sh`,
outside its exit criteria. Revisit only if a third bash consumer appears.
